WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.858
Welcome to lesson one: how LLM inference actually consumes a GPU.

2
00:00:05.188 --> 00:00:07.527
Every scaling answer in this domain starts here.

3
00:00:08.327 --> 00:00:12.187
Picture a lawyer pasting a sixty-page contract into an assistant,

4
00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:13.931
asking for the risky clauses.

5
00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:18.221
The screen sits blank for eight seconds, then the answer streams out smoothly.

6
00:00:19.021 --> 00:00:22.641
That pause and that stream are two phases with different bottlenecks.

7
00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:27.401
Prefill reads the whole prompt in parallel, saturating the GPU's compute.

8
00:00:28.201 --> 00:00:32.503
Prefill sets the time to first token, and its cost grows with prompt length.

9
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:36.836
So a long contract, or a long agent transcript, means a long wait.

10
00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:41.725
Decode then writes one token at a time, limited by memory bandwidth.

11
00:00:42.055 --> 00:00:46.399
It sets the gap between tokens, while GPU compute sits mostly idle.

12
00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:50.104
Because the phases stress different resources,

13
00:00:50.154 --> 00:00:53.059
NVIDIA Dynamo runs them on separate GPU pools.

14
00:00:53.389 --> 00:00:57.477
Each pool is sized on its own, and the KV cache is passed between them.

15
00:00:58.277 --> 00:01:02.899
Now fifty lawyers log in, and the GPU runs out of memory, though the weights fit.

16
00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:04.860
The culprit is the KV cache,

17
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.641
which stores keys and values for every token already processed.

18
00:01:09.441 --> 00:01:13.273
It grows with batch size, sequence length, layers, and hidden size.

19
00:01:13.603 --> 00:01:16.711
So the cache, not the weights, limits how many requests fit.

20
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:21.471
Agents hurt here, because a forty-step run is one very long sequence.

21
00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:23.799
The fixes are paged attention,

22
00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:27.554
FP8 cache quantization, prefix caching, and compaction.

23
00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:32.507
Static batching waits for a full batch, then stalls on the longest sequence.

24
00:01:32.837 --> 00:01:36.522
Continuous batching hands each finished slot to a queued request,

25
00:01:36.572 --> 00:01:38.419
for a multi-fold throughput gain.

26
00:01:39.219 --> 00:01:42.241
Batch size is the dial between throughput and latency.

27
00:01:42.571 --> 00:01:46.702
Bigger batches raise total tokens per second, but slow each request.

28
00:01:47.502 --> 00:01:52.380
NIM gives you continuous batching, prefix caching, and quantization out of the box.

29
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.841
But quantization is never free, so re-run your own evaluation first.

30
00:01:57.641 --> 00:02:01.168
Self-host NIM for data residency or steady volume;

31
00:02:01.218 --> 00:02:03.671
use a hosted API for spiky traffic.

32
00:02:04.001 --> 00:02:08.879
Both are OpenAI-compatible, so switching is usually a base URL change.

33
00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:14.600
On the exam, to hit a strict latency target, cap the batch size and add replicas.

34
00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.437
Now read on for the details.
